Electric Vehicles

They're just so much better for most cases, okay?

After riding with some colleagues' Teslas on visits to California, I bought my first EV, a Tesla Model S 90D, in 2017. (This was when Elon was just a creepy rich jerk, long before he showed his true jackass colors.) We were returning to the US after 12 years overseas, needed a new car, and I was in love with the idea of going electric. The "computer on wheels" thing was interesting, and early self-driving was mostly a novelty, especially in Vermont. But it looked great, it took off like a scared cat, it was very comfortable with four people, and I just plugged it in every night. Expensive but worth it, and it was, for four years.

In 2020 we traded it in for a Model Y Performance. Smaller and not as plush, but better sight lines and easier to load with stuff. Went even faster, too! From there we went to a 2024 Model Y LR, with a softer suspection. I tell you all this to present my electric car bona fides. I've been doing it for ten years.

Here are the reasons that an EV might not be for you:

- You regularly drive more than 300 miles a day and cannot imagine stopping for 30 minutes every 250 miles. Poor you, but you win.
- You drive mostly in extremely remote places. I mean more remote than the UP of Michigan. 
- You often tow a heavy trailer long distances.
That's about it. Those are really the only reasons I can imagine not just relaxing into the awesomeness of electric driving. Almost everything is so much better:

The driving experience is top notch compared to an ICEV. 
- Instant torque, as much as you want, no stupid downshifts, no lag, no loss of power above 40mph. 
- They are silent. If you want to make a statement, don't rev your engine like a dipshit, do 0-60mph in 3 seconds. Your passengers will make more noise than any engine.
- You don't need to start it or wait for it to warm up.
- The engine doesn't stop at lights to "save gas." God I hated that one.  
- You never need to go to a gas station again. Plug your car into the wall when you get it home, that's it. Unplug it when you leave. It's easy to get used to.
- No oil changes. No tune-ups. No transmission fluid. Brake service almost never. 

You might still have objections. Wait, you say, I heard that:
- EVs are actually worse for the environment than ICEVs. [Bullshit](https://theicct.org/why-evs-are-already-much-greener-than-combustion-engine-vehicles-jul25/).
- Oh but electricity is expensive where I live. [It's not more expensive than gas](https://coltura.org/ev-savings-report/).
- All that automation is tracking me. Welcome to the 21st century, all cars (oh yeah, and phones, and credit cards, and...) do this now. Buy [one of these](https://www.equinenow.com/under500.htm) instead.
- EVs are so much more expensive than ICEVs. Maybe, but it all depends on what you buy for either example. You can get a [new EV for $25k](https://www.slate.auto/en/blank-slate) (I'm getting one of these in May '26!), or you can spend over $100k. Talk to me again when gas is below $4/gal.
